        <body>
            <div xml:id="d261">

                <pb facs="0419" n="333" sameAs="frus:frus1927v01/pg333"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">721.2315/326: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Poindexter</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-01-12T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 12,
                                1927—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 9:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>4. President Leguía told the Colombian Minister that, due to the delicate
                    situation occasioned by the Tacna-Arica question, he would be unable to submit
                    the boundary treaty to the present Congress. The Colombian Minister replied
                    that, because of reiterated promises made by the President and the Foreign
                    Office on various specific occasions during the last few years and especially
                    during the last few months that the treaty would be submitted to Congress and
                    promptly ratified and the fact that they had failed to fulfill any of these
                    promises, he considered it useless to negotiate further on this subject with the
                    Government of Peru and he would not return again to discuss the subject with
                    him.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>The Colombian Minister also told President Leguía that if the treaty were not
                    submitted and approved by this Congress, a dangerous condition of public opinion
                    in Colombia would be created. President Leguía seemed greatly embarrassed and
                    assured the Colombian Minister that the present state of affairs was due to
                    circumstances beyond his control. He begged him to be patient, and again assured
                    him that his intention to have the treaty ratified at some future date was as
                    strong as ever. The Colombian Minister appears to have little confidence in this
                    and has informed his Government of the present state of affairs.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Poindexter</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
